t \\textrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\ngives the total work done by $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle from the initial point $\\mathbf{r}(a)$ to the terminal point $\\mathbf{r}(b)$ on the curve.\n\nNow suppose that $C$ is a *closed* curve. The **circulation** of $\\mathbf{F}$ along $C,$ denoted using the Greek letter $\\Gamma$ (pronounced \"gamma\"), is given by the line integral\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r = \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nRemember that the notation $\\displaystyle\\oint$ simply reminds us that we're integrating along a closed path.\n\nThe circulation $\\Gamma$ gives the total work done by $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along the entire closed path. We can also think of $\\Gamma$ as a measure of how much $\\mathbf F$ circulates around $C.$\n\nLet's build some intuition by considering some examples. \n\n- Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ and the closed curve $C$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction, shown below. \n\n![](images/9e/9e9de4e3734be4f7d8f59f6d54d0ed92c51db3a0beb8277f4b450531b7dab332.png)\n\nIt's clear from the picture that $\\mathbf{F}$ *assists* the motion of a particle as it moves counterclockwise along the path. Therefore, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r >    0.\n$$\n\n- Now imagine that $C$ is traversed in the clockwise direction.\n\n![](images/16/16dd701d51e3b43714b58993f66e42359289f557ef86fd9091ab6b8bba7041e7.png)\n\nWe see that $\\mathbf F$ works *against* the particle's motion as it moves clockwise along the path. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r < 0.\n$$\n\n- Finally, consider the vector field $\\mathbf{G}$ and the closed curve $C$ traversed in the clockwise direction, shown below.\n\n![](images/8b/8bf269cecceeefc3e81b868a4fc4c54127f00432145782b24719bb49552868c7.png)\n\nSince the force $\\mathbf G$ acts perpendicular to the particle's motion as it moves around the path, it neither helps nor hinders its motion. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf G\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180719 #edb/ref 1065 5833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180719 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4446c197-397c-5e41-a7a9-22d57126ffae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180719 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180721 #edb/ref 1064 "The fundamental theorem of line integrals states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a))\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is a piecewise-smooth curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and $f$ is differentiable function such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ \n\nNow, if $C$ is a closed path that's traversed once, then $\\mathbf r(a) = \\mathbf r(b),$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circulation of a conservative vector field around *any* closed path equals zero." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180721 #edb/ref 1065 5836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180721 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5bc343b6-5187-5353-bb7b-23db12eaa417" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180721 #edb/ref 1067 "Circulation in Conservative Vector Fields" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186454899 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186455515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186454899 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186455518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186454899 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186455519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186454899 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186455523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186454899 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186455533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186454899 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186454899 #edb/ref 1045 "Circulation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186454899 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186455