186455133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455404 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455404 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455404 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455404 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455404 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455404 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186455240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455404 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bbf611c1-7a64-56ff-a8ce-599f171848cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455405 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455405 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455405 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455405 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455405 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455405 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455405 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455405 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186455223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455405 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bd93af99-5bab-55c2-8a85-e0d5efec7517" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455420 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455420 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455420 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455420 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455420 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455420 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455420 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455420 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186455176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455420 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e2afbdbe-c2b8-5f8b-8cd1-c52860a3b3d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455447 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455447 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125482" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455447 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455447 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ oriented counterclockwise." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455447 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "442b3fed-648b-5cc8-977b-6700ae26738b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455447 #edb/ref 1074 "The closed curve $C$ is the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455463 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455463 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125667" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455463 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455463 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/13/13e4c6a06a44099024ce91ef6c9ed4679af037c2e794351597a4d604bf39dc6f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nII. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nIII. ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455463 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "674f4764-9a63-51ae-b803-b7fdf438bb38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455463 #edb/ref 1074 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/63/6371bb04d33719ee18c65a92354e84be0e9077e780eac9559dcb7ec42deb8d99.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](images/0b/0bd9476fa9b19f04b40fbb9cca2986ed90495aa134307606622ef91070af19a7.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455485 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455485 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125640" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455485 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455485 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a1/a1883ee3a3a99d43109bae2d2093aef35548dde9753cfb7dc19dfd77a104ec83.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nIII. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455485 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a9318e5b-e833-584c-92bb-558dab7e94de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455485 #edb/ref 1074 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/85/8595904122fac3431fc959b2fa4aa5a3062bd1bffd1a391a0861fa12f2498054.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves **clockwise** along $C.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1f31f2b6eefddedc46b8e845703904c0b4566963e64a644f7a4ab32bb7240c3.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455487 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455487 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125666" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455487 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455487 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d0/d042734a8c7483c54795cd6ec8527b43f398cb77487d054a36b27d704f27a318.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nII. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n\nIII. ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455487 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "afd2a4b0-18e7-5bc4-9563-64bdaef16530" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455487 #edb/ref 1074 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves clockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/f8/f8268167bb50f9d03cb38a3d505fa6b40003e307b149356089f5b0dc3dd32110.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$\n\n![](images/a8/a83934aa58040b4eb2bb957d1d12caba676e306e41086480d064935eab9f1c59.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455499 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455499 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125669" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455499 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455499 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/36/36ebce64036b7661c81aa6525c407f698375544532985175cc465ddc3ff4f5a8.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nII. ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nIII. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455499 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d78a254d-adb2-5551-a797-0a608524ca4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455499 #edb/ref 1074 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while sta